    description: 'Grooming, hoof care, and the role of injuries in everyday stable life.',
    answer: 'Horse care includes brushing, combing, hoof picking, and treating injuries. The official store connects grooming and hoof care with preventing problems such as saddle sores and abscesses.',
    sections: [['Read the care screen', 'Inspect your horse’s condition and the available care tools. Official screenshots show a dedicated horse care view with access to feeding, grooming, tack, and medical options. Store screenshots can show an earlier build, so labels may differ in your copy.'], ['Grooming and hooves', 'Brushing and using the hoof pick are functional parts of caring for a horse. Learn what each tool does in the game and use the in-game guidance when a problem appears. This wiki does not claim a fixed number of brush strokes or a proven daily tool order.'], ['Injuries and recovery', 'Scrapes and other injuries can require treatment. Read the game’s medical information for the affected horse before deciding what to do next. Exact treatment items, recovery times, and hidden injury chances have not been verified.'], ['Keep the herd manageable', 'As an editorial recommendation, make sure you can keep up with care before growing the herd. Staff can help with a larger yard, but their precise task coverage should be checked in your game.'], ['Daily routine', 'According to the community wiki, each day you put out hay and water for every stable group and muck out. Water is not shown as a stat, but horses get thirsty without it. Grooming means dragging brushes over the dirty areas, and treating a wound means dragging the right medication onto it.'], ['Illnesses and injuries in the game', 'The community wiki lists these conditions: cut, infected cut, abrasion, infected abrasion, lameness, saddle sores, tendon injury, dirty and sick, malnourished, laminitis, and thrush with hoof abscess. Each needs the right treatment, either from your house pharmacy or from the vet. The in-game library book Injuries & Illnesses explains them. Falls raise the risk of injury, and vet bills are higher for neglected or injury-prone horses.']],
    faq: [['Does grooming affect gameplay?', 'Yes. The store explicitly connects care tools with avoiding certain health problems.'], ['What happens if I neglect my horses?', 'The developer FAQ says neglected horses can be taken away by animal welfare. A confiscated horse is effectively out of the game.'], ['Where do I buy more hay and feed?', 'A player reply under the official FAQ says: go to the city from the home map, enter the store (the building on the right with the saddle sign), and click the hay and food behind the clerk. This is a community answer, not developer documentation.'], ['What is the correct treatment for every injury?', 'A complete, verified treatment table is not available here yet. Consult the in-game medical information.'], ['Can staff help with care?', 'The official store confirms that staff assist with care and training, without publishing a complete task table.'], ['Which illnesses are in Horses of Hoofprint Bay?', 'The community wiki lists cuts, abrasions (both can become infected), lameness, saddle sores, tendon injuries, dirty and sick, malnourished, laminitis, and thrush with hoof abscess.'], ['Two brushes are stuck to my mouse. Is that a bug?', 'Yes. v1.0.2 fixed selecting two draggable items at once, so update the game.']],
